---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import { checked, contradictions, healthLinked, ranked, SELLS_LABEL, stats } from './_privacy';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'CollectionPage',
  name: 'What cycle apps say they do with your data',
  description: `Every claim taken from the ${stats.total} apps' own App Store privacy labels and their own privacy policies, with a link and a quote for each.`,
  url: 'https://niyora.com/compare/privacy/',
};
---

<Page
  title="What cycle apps say they do with your data · Niyora"
  description={`${stats.tracked} of ${stats.total} cycle apps declare they track you across other companies' apps. Taken from their own App Store labels and privacy policies, checked ${checked}.`}
  canonicalUrl="https://niyora.com/compare/privacy/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="s hero">
    <div class="eyebrow">Compare</div>
    <h1>What cycle apps say they do with your data</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Not what I think they do. What they told Apple, and what they wrote in their own privacy
      policies. Every cell below links to the source it came from, so you can check any of it
      yourself.
    </p>
    <p class="meta">Checked on {checked}.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="s">
    <div class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="stat-num">{stats.tracked} of {stats.total}</div>
        <p>
          tell Apple their data may be used to track you across apps and websites owned by other
          companies.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="stat-num">{stats.healthLinked}</div>
        <p>
          declare that health or sensitive information is linked to your identity, not held
          anonymously.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="stat-num">{stats.deletionReachesShared}</div>
        <p>
          says deleting your account also pulls back the data it already sent to other companies.
          One, out of {stats.total}.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="s">
    <h2>The table</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      Ordered by how many kinds of data each app tells Apple may be used to track you, then by
      whether health data is linked to your identity. Fewest first.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">App</th>
            <th scope="col">Tracks you across other companies</th>
            <th scope="col">Health linked to your identity</th>
            <th scope="col">Where your health data lives</th>
            <th scope="col">Sells data</th>
            <th scope="col">Deletion reaches data already shared</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {ranked.map((a) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                <a href={a.labelSource} rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">{a.app}</a>
                <span class="sub">{a.jurisdiction}</span>
              </th>
              <td class={a.tracking.length ? 'bad' : 'good'}>
                {a.tracking.length ? a.tracking.join(', ') : 'Declares none'}
              </td>
              <td class={healthLinked(a) ? 'bad' : 'good'}>
                {healthLinked(a) ? 'Yes' : 'Not declared'}
              </td>
              <td>
                {a.storage}
                <a class="cite" href={a.storageSource} rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">source</a>
              </td>
              <td class={a.sells === 'no' ? 'good' : a.sells === 'unknown' ? '' : 'bad'}>
                {SELLS_LABEL[a.sells]}
                <a class="cite" href={a.sellsSource} rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">source</a>
              </td>
              <td class={a.deletionCoversShared === 'yes' ? 'good' : ''}>
                {a.deletionCoversShared === 'yes'
                  ? 'Yes'
                  : a.deletionCoversShared === 'no'
                    ? 'No'
                    : 'Not addressed'}
                <a class="cite" href={a.deletionSource} rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">source</a>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      "Declares none" means the app's App Store label has no "Data Used to Track You" section. It
      is their declaration, not my audit.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="s">
    <h2>If someone asks for your data</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      A court order is the case where the difference between these policies stops being abstract.
      Here is what each one commits to, in its own words.
    </p>
    <dl class="law-list">
      {ranked.map((a) => (
        <div class="law-row">
          <dt>{a.app}</dt>
          <dd>
            {a.lawEnforcement}
            <a class="cite" href={a.lawSource} rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">source</a>
          </dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="s">
    <h2>Where their own sources disagree</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      Each app declares itself three times: to Apple, to Google, and in its own policy. Those
      three do not always match. Every one of these is the company against itself.
    </p>
    <ol class="contras">
      {contradictions.map((c) => (
        <li class="contra">
          <div class="contra-app">{c.app}</div>
          <p>{c.summary}</p>
          <p class="contra-src">
            {c.sources.map((s, i) => (
              <a href={s} rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">source {i + 1}</a>
            ))}
          </p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="s">
    <h2>Where Niyora sits, held to the same standard</h2>
    <div class="science">
      <p>
        Niyora is not in the table above, and I want to be straight about why. It is not publicly
        listed on the App Store yet, so it has no App Store privacy label. You cannot check my
        claims the same way you can check theirs. Take what follows as my statement, not as an
        independent declaration.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no account, so there is nothing to tie your writing to a name. Your history stays
        in the app's private storage on your phone, and the text you wrote is encrypted with
        AES-256 using a key held in the iOS Keychain that does not sync to iCloud or to backups.
        There is no copy on a server I control, because there is no such server.
      </p>
      <p>
        One thing does leave your phone. To turn what you wrote into a reflection, the text is
        sent to Google's Vertex AI. Before it goes, an automatic scrub swaps certain personal
        details for stand-ins, and swaps them back in the reply. That scrub is careful but not
        exhaustive: a bare name with no cue around it will go through, and the emotional content
        of what you wrote always goes through. Google also sees your device's IP address, as it
        would with any request. Under the enterprise terms, that text is not used to train
        Google's models and is not reviewed by a human by default.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the honest version is not "nothing leaves your phone". It is "one thing leaves, you can
        read exactly what and why, and none of it is tied to an account".
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href="/privacy/" class="btn ghost">Read the full privacy policy</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="s">
    <h2>How I checked this</h2>
    <div class="science">
      <p>
        Three source types only: each app's Apple App Store privacy label, its Google Play data
        safety entry, and its own privacy policy on its own domain. No news articles, no reviews,
        no reputation. Where a source did not answer a question, the cell says so instead of
        guessing.
      </p>
      <p>
        These labels are self-declared. Apple and Google do not verify them, and a label can be
        out of date. That cuts both ways: an app may be doing less than its label suggests, or
        more. What the table shows is what each company chose to declare, on {checked}.
      </p>
      <p>
        I left out Cara Care, which appears elsewhere on this site as a comparison. Its iOS and
        Android builds have not been updated since October 2020, it carries no App Store privacy
        label at all, and it is a gut-health tracker rather than a cycle app. Putting it in this
        table would have padded the count without telling you anything true.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href="/research/" class="btn ghost">The research behind Niyora</a>
      <a href="/quiz/" class="btn">Take the quiz</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  /* Page-specific only: the stat cards, the data table, the law list, the
     contradiction cards and the "science" prose blocks. Shared chrome (sheet,
     sections, dividers, type scale, buttons, footer) comes from Page's global CSS. */
  .meta { font-size: 14px; margin-top: 18px; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.45); }
  .note { font-size: 14px; margin-top: 20px; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.45); }

  .stat-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 14px;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 940px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    text-align: left;
  }
  .stat {
    padding: 26px 24px;
    border-radius: 18px;
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.03);
  }
  .stat-num {
    font-size: 44px;
    font-weight: 300;
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -1px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.96);
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }
  .stat p { font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.55; max-width: none; }

  .table-scroll {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 1000px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: 22px auto 0;
    -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
  }
  table { width: 100%; min-width: 860px; border-collapse: collapse; text-align: left; font-size: 14px; }
  th, td {
    padding: 13px 14px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
    font-weight: 300;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72);
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  thead th {
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 1.5px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.42);
    font-weight: 400;
  }
  tbody th { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.92); font-weight: 400; white-space: nowrap; }
  tbody th a { color: inherit; text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }
  .sub {
    display: block;
    font-size: 11px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.38);
    margin-top: 4px;
    font-weight: 300;
    white-space: normal;
  }
  td.good { color: hsl(152, 45%, 72%); }
  td.bad { color: hsl(20, 60%, 74%); }
  .cite {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-left: 8px;
    font-size: 11px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }
  .cite:hover { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85); }

  .law-list { width: 100%; max-width: 860px; text-align: left; margin: 22px auto 0; }
  .law-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 190px 1fr;
    gap: 18px;
    padding: 15px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
  }
  .law-row dt { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9); font-size: 15px; }
  .law-row dd { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66); font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.55; margin: 0; }
  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .law-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 6px; }
  }

  .contras {
    list-style: none;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 860px;
    text-align: left;
    margin: 24px auto 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 14px;
  }
  .contra {
    padding: 22px 22px 18px;
    border-radius: 18px;
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.03);
  }
  .contra-app {
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 3px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: rgba(var(--accent-rgb), 0.9);
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .contra p { max-width: none; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.6; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.82); }
  .contra-src { margin-top: 12px; }
  .contra-src a {
    font-size: 12px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.4);
    margin-right: 14px;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
  .contra-src a:hover { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9); }

  .science {
    max-width: 740px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 32px 30px;
    border-radius: 20px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(var(--accent-rgb), 0.24);
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.03);
    text-align: left;
  }
  .science p { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.62; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); max-width: none; margin: 0 0 16px; }
  .science p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .cta-row { display: flex; gap: 16px; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: center; margin-top: 28px; }

  @media (max-width: 900px) { .stat-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .s { padding: 56px 24px; }
    h1 { font-size: 34px; }
    h2 { font-size: 26px; }
  }
</style>
